; host/decimal_inspect.bl — the pinned PROTOCOL IMPL for `BeamLisp.Decimal`'s
; `Inspect` protocol.
;
; `defimpl Inspect, for: BeamLisp.Decimal` is its own module,
; `Inspect.BeamLisp.Decimal`, compiled beside the type it serves. This file is
; the `.bl` port of that impl, compiled by `BeamLisp.AOT/compile_host_file`
; into `Elixir.Inspect.BeamLisp.Decimal.beam`.
;
; THE MODULE ATOM IS OUTSIDE `Elixir.BeamLisp.*` — `Inspect.<Type>` — the same
; case `priv/host/lazy_seq_inspect.bl` and `priv/host/vector_enumerable.bl`
; carry: a name-prefix rule cannot name this beam, so the seed's host inventory
; carries the mapping as data.
;
; ABI PARITY IS THE CONTRACT, and it is why this impl is PORTED rather than
; dropped: without it a decimal falls back to `Inspect.Any` and renders as an
; opaque struct map, losing the `1.50M` literal face a human reads — including
; inside a test failure message, where the difference between `1.50M` and
; `%BeamLisp.Decimal{unscaled: 150, scale: 2}` is the difference between a
; readable failure and a puzzle. `inspect/2` answers exactly what the live impl
; answered — the decimal's reader literal, `opts` IGNORED (the live body is
; `BeamLisp.Decimal.to_literal(d)`, so `limit:`/`pretty:` change nothing);
; `__impl__/1` answers `:for` (the type) and `:protocol` (the protocol), and an
; unknown key raises the `FunctionClauseError` the generated two-clause function
; raised. The module exports exactly the live pair.
;
; ONE BY-DESIGN DIFFERENCE, the one every pinned impl carries: the live
; `__impl__/1` is INLINED by the protocol compiler, so its refusal names
; `"-inlined-__impl__/1-"` while this plain function names `__impl__`. The
; CLASS, the arity and the module are identical; only the internal function
; name differs, and the test normalizes it (the same axis
; `test/bl/host_vector_test.bl` folds).
;
; NOT ON THE BOOT PATH. An `Inspect` impl is reached only through `Kernel.inspect`
; / `Inspect.*`, never while the compiler or the reader is interning a tier, so
; this file uses the ordinary beam-lisp surface (`(tuple …)`, `(list …)`, `=`)
; exactly as `priv/host/lazy_seq_inspect.bl` does. Its only beam-lisp
; dependencies are `BeamLisp.Ns.Core` and the type module it serves, which is
; called at its PRODUCTION atom by construction: the impl dispatches BY the
; value's tag, so the values it is reached with are production-tagged decimals.
;
; FUNCTIONS-ONLY: no `:struct_fields`, so this beam gains no `__struct__` or
; `__info__` export — the same divergence the live `defimpl` module does not
; carry, and for the reason the sibling impls give: the generated `__info__/1` a
; live `defimpl` answers is `module_info` pass-through metadata
; (`:attributes`/`:compile`/`:md5`), which the pinned emitter deliberately does
; not synthesize. `__impl__`'s error names its own module from a fun literal
; compiled into THIS file, so at a probe atom it answers that atom —
; module-exact, never a hard-coded `Inspect.BeamLisp.Decimal`.
;
; (This wave owns source and tests only; the seed entry that points this file at
; `Elixir.Inspect.BeamLisp.Decimal` is written by the wave that deletes
; `decimal.ex`, the family convention every pinned port in `priv/host/` follows.)
;
; Run: bin/bl test test/bl/host_decimal_test.bl

(ns host.decimal-inspect)

(defn __impl__
  "The `defimpl`-generated introspection: `:for` is the type, `:protocol` the
   protocol. An unknown key is the `FunctionClauseError` the generated
   two-clause function raised."
  [k]
  (cond
    (= :for k) :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Decimal"
    (= :protocol k) :"Elixir.Inspect"
    :else
      (erlang/error
        (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
          (list (tuple :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))
                (tuple :function :__impl__)
                (tuple :arity 1))))))

(defn inspect
  "`Inspect.inspect/2`: the decimal's reader literal, with the `M` suffix that
   makes it read back as the same value — `1.50M`, scale and all. The options
   are ignored, exactly as the live impl ignored them."
  [d _opts]
  (BeamLisp.Decimal/to_literal d))
